Sogen: the calls that re-enable a WSAEventSelect event re-enable it

WSAEventSelect records each network event once, until the call that
re-enables it: an accept for FD_ACCEPT, a receive for FD_READ, a send
for FD_WRITE. If the condition still holds then -- another connection
waiting, more data -- the event is recorded and signaled again. Sogen's
AFD cleared its recorded events only on WSAEnumNetworkEvents and a new
WSAEventSelect, so an event recorded again after the enumeration, before
the re-enabling call, stayed recorded with nothing to clear it, and its
next occurrence was never signaled.

usocket's wait-for-input on Windows waits on its WSAEventSelect event,
enumerating the socket's events only when that is signaled, and
hunchentoot's listener waits there for each connection; under Sogen a
GET from the process to its own hunchentoot was never accepted.

Accept, the stream and datagram receives (ReadFile on a socket
included) and the sends now clear the event they re-enable.

